**Answer: The assertion is TRUE.**

**Proof.**

Let $X$ be a normed vector space and $T : X \to \mathbb{R}$ an unbounded (i.e., non-bounded) linear operator. Unboundedness means

$$\sup_{\|x\|\le 1} |Tx| = +\infty.$$

Fix an arbitrary $\alpha \in \mathbb{R}^+$ (so $\alpha > 0$). We construct a sequence $\{x_n\} \subseteq X$ with $x_n \to 0$ and $Tx_n \to \alpha$.

**Construction.** Since $\sup_{\|x\|\le 1}|Tx| = \infty$, for each $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $n \ge 1$ there exists $y_n \in X$ with

$$\|y_n\| \le 1 \quad\text{and}\quad |Ty_n| \ge n.$$

In particular $Ty_n \neq 0$ for every $n$. Define

$$x_n := \frac{\alpha}{Ty_n}\, y_n.$$

**Verification.**

*Linearity gives a constant value:*

$$Tx_n = T\!\left(\frac{\alpha}{Ty_n} y_n\right) = \frac{\alpha}{Ty_n}\, Ty_n = \alpha \quad\text{for all } n.$$

Hence $Tx_n \to \alpha$ (in fact $Tx_n = \alpha$ identically).

*Convergence to zero:*

$$\|x_n\| = \left\|\frac{\alpha}{Ty_n} y_n\right\| = \frac{\alpha}{|Ty_n|}\,\|y_n\| \le \frac{\alpha}{n} \xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{} 0.$$

Thus $x_n \to 0$ in $X$.

**Conclusion.** For every $\alpha > 0$ we have produced a sequence $\{x_n\} \subseteq X$ with $x_n \to 0$ and $Tx_n \to \alpha$. This is exactly the claimed property, and it holds for an arbitrary unbounded linear operator. $\blacksquare$

**Remark.** The construction exploits the defining feature of unboundedness: the operator takes arbitrarily large values on the unit ball. Rescaling each $y_n$ by the factor $\alpha / Ty_n$ both forces the image to the target value $\alpha$ and shrinks the norm to at most $\alpha/n$, sending the sequence to $0$. This is the mechanism by which an unbounded linear operator fails to be continuous at the origin — and indeed fails in the strongest possible way, realizing every positive real number as a limit of images of a null sequence.

### PROOF COMPLETE
